          "say": "The side of a square with area 10 is between 3 and 4.",
          "write": "3 < √10 < 4"
        },
        {
          "say": "You can never write it exactly as a fraction. That's a surd.",
          "write": "surd"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Rational and irrational",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "A rational number can be written as a fraction of two integers.",
          "write": "3/4,  −5,  0.333… = 1/3"
        },
        {
          "say": "Its decimal either stops or repeats.",
          "write": "stops or repeats"
        },
        {
          "say": "An irrational number goes on forever with no repeating pattern.",
          "write": "π,  √2 = 1.414 213 56…"
        },
        {
          "say": "A surd is a root of a whole number that is irrational.",
          "write": "√2, √3, √10, √50"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Is it a surd?",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Root 49 is 7 and root 100 is 10. Those are not surds.",
          "write": "√49 = 7  ✗ surd"
        },
        {
          "say": "Quick test: if it's not a perfect square under the root, it's a surd.",
          "write": "not a perfect square → surd"
        },
        {
          "say": "A square with area 2 has sides of root 2. Root 2 times root 2 is 2.",
          "write": "√2 × √2 = 2",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y9/maths/introduction-to-surds-square.svg",
          "alt": "A square with area 2 cm² and sides √2 cm"
        },
        {
          "say": "Is root 45 a surd? 36 and 49 are squares, but 45 isn't. Yes.",
          "write": "6² = 36,  7² = 49"
        }
      ]
    },
